Excellent — you’re almost done! 🙌  

The errors you’re now seeing are **TypeScript test-spec errors** (they are coming from the `*.spec.ts` files).  
These occur because your **directive’s constructors expect injected parameters** (like `ElementRef`, `Renderer2`), but the auto‑generated test files call them **without any arguments**.

In other words:

Your directive has something like:
```ts
constructor(private el: ElementRef, private renderer: Renderer2) {}
```

but the spec file has:
```ts
const directive = new ScaleDirective();
```

Hence 👉 **“Expected 2 arguments, but got 0.”**

---

### ✅ You have 3 easy options to fix this

---

## 🟢 **Option 1 (Simplest & Recommended for Now) — Delete Spec Files**

If you are not actively writing or running Angular unit tests right now (which most beginners aren’t during setup), you can safely delete these spec files:

```
src/app/login/Directives/blink.spec.ts
src/app/login/Directives/rotate.spec.ts
src/app/login/Directives/scale.spec.ts
src/app/login/Directives/shadow.spec.ts
src/app/login/Directives/highlight.spec.ts
```

Do it from VS Code (Right‑click → Delete).

These files are **only used for Jasmine unit testing** — deleting them will not affect your Angular app build or serve.

---

## 🟡 **Option 2 — Fix Spec Files**

If you do want to keep them for unit testing, fix the constructor calls like this:

Example → **blink.spec.ts**
```ts
import { ElementRef } from '@angular/core';
import { BlinkDirective } from './blink.directive';

describe('BlinkDirective', () => {
  it('should create an instance', () => {
    const elementRef = new ElementRef(document.createElement('div'));
    const directive = new BlinkDirective(elementRef);
    expect(directive).toBeTruthy();
  });
});
```

Example → **rotate.spec.ts**
```ts
import { ElementRef, Renderer2 } from '@angular/core';
import { RotateDirective } from './rotate.directive';

describe('RotateDirective', () => {
  it('should create an instance', () => {
    const elementRef = new ElementRef(document.createElement('div'));
    const mockRenderer = { setStyle: () => {} } as Renderer2;
    const directive = new RotateDirective(elementRef, mockRenderer);
    expect(directive).toBeTruthy();
  });
});
```

Do the same for **scale**, **shadow**, and **highlight**, adjusting constructor parameters (add `Renderer2` if needed).

---

## 🔴 **Option 3 — Ignore Spec Errors in Build**

If your goal is simply to run the app (not tests), you can also temporarily disable spec compilation:

Open `angular.json` and remove or comment out the `test` configuration section or use:

```bash
ng serve
```

instead of `ng test`.

`ng serve` doesn’t compile spec files by default unless you’ve modified something unusual.

---

### 🧹 Quick Summary

| Option | What it does | Recommended for |
|--------|---------------|----------------|
| 🟢 1. Delete spec files | Skips testing, removes TS errors | Most beginners / app dev |
| 🟡 2. Fix spec files | Lets you run proper unit tests | If you’re learning testing |
| 🔴 3. Ignore tests | Quick skip | Temporary workaround |

---

### 💡 Pro tip
Also rename your `Directives` folder to lowercase **`directives`**, because imports like  
`'./directives/blink.directive'` are case‑sensitive on Linux/macOS builds.

---

After doing **Option 1 (delete spec files)**, then run again:

```bash
ng serve --open
```

✅ Result → all TypeScript “Expected arguments” errors disappear, and your Angular app should compile successfully.
